page('10 / Causal interpretation and next evidence',[
P('Elevated prices can depress purchasing power even when inflation falls. A title that emphasizes that hardship can be accurate. Text measures price-level wording, but it cannot distinguish rational household experience from irrational anchoring without corresponding belief data.'),
P('Similarly, available negative stories do not prove availability bias in a reader. Confirmation bias requires evidence about prior beliefs and selective consumption. Our title data supply neither. Published Michigan group tables motivate heterogeneity checks, but do not link those groups to our sources.'),
P('Michigan reports partisan differences and differences between phone and web surveys [2]. We retain aggregate income, age, and party source tables for descriptive work. We do not estimate individual moderators from national monthly data. Our era indicators absorb many coincident changes and cannot isolate political persuasion or a survey-method effect.'),
P('Proposed truthful-framing experiment','Sub'),P('After required institutional review, recruit 600 consenting US adults. Randomize within income, baseline sentiment, and party strata. Assign 200 participants to price-level emphasis, inflation-rate emphasis, or balanced emphasis. Every summary contains the same verified values, dates, definitions, and source label from one official release.'),
P('Measure baseline household strain and beliefs before treatment. Record immediate economic expectations, perceived purchasing power, factual recall, and a sentiment-related response scale after treatment. Use the level-versus-rate contrast as the primary test. Estimate intention-to-treat effects with baseline adjustment and report confidence intervals.'),
P('Prespecify recruitment, exclusions, missing-data handling, attrition, secondary tests, and a seven-day retention check. Correct secondary comparisons. With 200 per arm, an independent-outcome normal approximation gives 80% power near 0.28 standard deviations at a two-sided 5% level. This is a planning calculation, not an observed effect.'),
P('Randomization addresses selective exposure within the experiment. Demand effects, noncompliance, spillovers, and artificial exposure remain risks. Moving from a vignette response to the national Michigan index requires representative sampling, exposure frequency, weighting, and transport assumptions.'),
P('The present study follows the permitted observational-analysis-plus-causal-proposal route. No experiment ran. Economic shocks, audience demand, and reverse selection prevent a causal interpretation of the current regressions.')])
